Emily: And so I think rather than just, you know, harping on him to try to get him to play this game, we figured we'd we'd just jump ahead.

9
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:55,840
Rym: And because we already played it and it was fantastic.

10
00:00:56,081 --> 00:00:56,644
Rym: And it's it's.

11
00:00:56,765 --> 00:00:59,720
Rym: it's a game that I highly recommend that GeekNights listeners play.

12
00:01:00,061 --> 00:01:08,160
Emily: It was it was funny because you basically did the thing that I just described where you played it and then you were on my case for like a couple of weeks.

13
00:01:08,241 --> 00:01:10,260
Emily: Be like, oh, my gosh, you have to play this game.

14
00:01:10,381 --> 00:01:12,700
Emily: I want to talk about this game with somebody.

15
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:17,280
Emily: I have to, you know, see your thoughts on it and see what decisions you make.

16
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:22,260
Emily: And the only other game I can remember you doing that with was Disco Elysium.

17
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:34,980
Emily: And I one thing that I really have been liking is there is a. it's not really a genre of game, but there's a certain type of game that I call couch games because they're not real time.

18
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:37,340
Emily: They're not like super competitive.

19
00:01:37,482 --> 00:01:38,579
Emily: They're more narrative based.

20
00:01:39,183 --> 00:01:42,440
Emily: And so we both play them kind of together.

21
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:54,420
Emily: Usually one person's kind of driving it and the other one is either spectating or, you know, contributing like we played Night in the Woods, basically where we both decide what to do together.

22
00:01:55,181 --> 00:02:00,936
Emily: And so that was how I played half of Disco Elysium, which you had already played.

23
00:02:01,017 --> 00:02:01,219
Emily: Right.

24
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:05,500
Emily: And that was how we played Night in the Woods.

25
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:09,900
Emily: And this is like the third game we've really done the couch gaming with.

26
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:13,820
Rym: So it's it's Slay the Princess, which I think I mentioned a few times on Geek Night.

27
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:15,479
Rym: So I think it's very good.

28
00:02:16,123 --> 00:02:22,380
Rym: For those of you who are unaware, it's a recent game and it was put out by Black Tabby Games, which was co-founded by Abby Howard.

29
00:02:22,842 --> 00:02:29,180
Rym: Now, you may remember Abby Howard as the famous strip search contestant who she's gone on to make games.

30
00:02:29,261 --> 00:02:30,179
Rym: And this is one of her games.

31
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:32,954
Rym: It is like 17 bucks on Steam.

32
00:02:32,974 --> 00:02:33,620
Rym: Full disclosure.

33
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:37,160
Rym: It's not the sort of cheapo indie game that we often talk about.

34
00:02:37,340 --> 00:02:38,819
Rym: I think that's part of why Scott won't play it.

35
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:44,100
Rym: But honestly, 20 bucks, 17 bucks for a game like this is a reasonable price.

36
00:02:44,422 --> 00:02:46,019
Rym: I got that much entertainment out of it.

37
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:52,759
Rym: And if you want to support indie games that are charging what it costs to make them, this is a better way to go.

38
00:02:53,240 --> 00:03:01,480
Emily: I mean, I guess what I thought about it like is it costs at least 20 dollars to get an indie published graphic novel.

39
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:05,640
Rym: You know, even even we paid 20 bucks each just to see Deadpool.

40
00:03:05,801 --> 00:03:10,080
Rym: And that was far less entertainment per minute total than this game was.

41
00:03:10,861 --> 00:03:17,720
Emily: And and think about like, you know, you will pay five bucks for a black and white zine that has interesting story.

42
00:03:18,282 --> 00:03:26,260
Emily: But this has like since it's so branching, it's like a zine, but also a tree of zines.

43
00:03:26,461 --> 00:03:34,220
Emily: And so you're paying paying like 20 bucks for a tree of of indie comic zines.

44
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:39,080
Rym: So the game will review it first pretty briefly, because the game is very straightforward.

45
00:03:39,301 --> 00:03:40,060
Rym: It's a very simple game.

46
00:03:40,101 --> 00:03:41,280
Rym: And then we're going to worry about spoilers.

47
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:44,559
Rym: Then we're going to talk about the game as we've played it.

48
00:03:45,446 --> 00:03:46,319
Emily: Just straight up.

49
00:03:46,540 --> 00:03:51,100
Emily: The title really gives away what the game is about on its surface.

50
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:56,720
Emily: It's like the cliche of saving the princess is basically turned on its head.

51
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:03,060
Emily: And you are asked from the first moments of the game to slay the princess.

52
00:04:03,581 --> 00:04:06,720
Rym: It's it's framed like an adventure game visual novel.

53
00:04:07,180 --> 00:04:10,780
Rym: You literally like you're on a road leading to a cabin.

54
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:12,131
Rym: There is a princess in the cabin.

55
00:04:12,151 --> 00:04:13,180
Rym: There's a knife in the cabin.

56
00:04:13,523 --> 00:04:15,480
Rym: You've got to go kill that princess.

57
00:04:16,100 --> 00:04:16,839
Rym: Don't ask questions.

58
00:04:17,463 --> 00:04:20,279
Rym: And the entire game is just reacting to that prompt.

59
00:04:20,579 --> 00:04:41,380
Emily: The narrator, because you start in a forest and the narrator, it's very similar to, you know, I worked on Colossal Cave remake with the Williamses and the type of narration is very similar to how it it plays in those old you know, technically it was originally text based.

60
00:04:41,581 --> 00:04:51,300
Emily: But then when they they do it with voiceover, it's often like a voice is telling you things and you're supposed to take in information from the voice.

61
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:58,539
Rym: But it's also very evocative of the Stanley parable in that the narrator is also narrating what you're doing and reacting to you.

62
00:04:59,080 --> 00:05:06,699
Rym: And the narrator, much like the Stanley parable, is a character with its own motivations and unreliability is.

63
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:10,220
Rym: Well, I mean, maybe maybe you believe the narrator or not.

64
00:05:10,460 --> 00:05:12,739
Rym: Maybe you trust what that character says or not.

65
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:16,640
Rym: But the narrator is motivated and a part of the story.

66
00:05:17,262 --> 00:05:22,920
Rym: And the whole game is just very simple, sketchy, semi animated drawings.

67
00:05:23,180 --> 00:05:25,239
Rym: You know, Abby Howard did all the art, from what I can tell.

68
00:05:26,120 --> 00:05:27,660
Rym: It's got some pretty good music in the background.

69
00:05:28,221 --> 00:05:32,500
Rym: It's got two voice actors and it won't take you that long to be.

70
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:34,560
Rym: This is like an afternoon's worth of play.

71
00:05:35,440 --> 00:05:50,020
Emily: Yeah, I think one thing that I was kind of struck about was the fact that it has a kind of, you know, it's got this very like I made the comparison to indie comics.

72
00:05:50,122 --> 00:05:51,159
Emily: It's all black and white.

73
00:05:51,580 --> 00:05:55,259
Emily: And, you know, it does feel like a web comic or something like that.

74
00:05:55,621 --> 00:06:00,299
Emily: But it's got this very fascinating effect that they even warn you about it.

75
00:06:01,062 --> 00:06:04,999
Rym: Well, the game's booting up a good amount of accessibility features for a visual novel.

76
00:06:05,100 --> 00:06:09,120
Emily: Yeah, they're like some people get dizzy from the parallax effect.

77
00:06:09,322 --> 00:06:11,020
Emily: So, you know, keep that in mind.

78
00:06:11,262 --> 00:06:13,020
Emily: You know, we don't want you to get motion sick.

79
00:06:13,581 --> 00:06:17,740
Emily: But I found it to be really, really an interesting.

80
00:06:18,142 --> 00:06:29,200
Emily: It just added that little bit of visual polish to the game where there's like a certain amount of parallax between the foreground elements and the background elements and the world kind of shifts.

81
00:06:30,187 --> 00:06:30,898
Emily: And, you know, there's.

82
00:06:32,222 --> 00:06:46,920
Emily: there are some other artistic effects they add later in the game that I think they're very simple, but they add not just to the visuals, but also kind of set a tone or mood to certain scenes.

83
00:06:47,682 --> 00:06:52,620
Rym: So, yeah, I mean, it's categorized like in genre wise, like on Steam.

84
00:06:52,721 --> 00:06:55,318
Rym: It's called a dating sim, which I can see.

85
00:06:56,467 --> 00:06:57,259
Rym: I can see why.

86
00:06:57,581 --> 00:07:00,600
Rym: Partly there are some endings that are possible that we didn't explore.

87
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:06,060
Rym: Yeah, new psychological horror with the specific subnote of body horror.

88
00:07:06,362 --> 00:07:08,460
Emily: Yes, there's definitely body horror.

89
00:07:09,101 --> 00:07:14,704
Emily: And one thing that I think, you know, this game, it should.

90
00:07:15,066 --> 00:07:19,420
Emily: it's not super gruesome, but it definitely should.

91
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:22,938
Emily: If you have a lot of, I don't know.

92
00:07:24,521 --> 00:07:33,419
Emily: Kind of reaction to gore, like you need a trigger warning for something like, you know, seeing a bone, you know, maybe.

93
00:07:34,220 --> 00:07:45,839
Rym: It's a sketchy smile, but there is a there can potentially be a lot of gore and death for what is a visual novel of someone sent to slay a princess in the cabin in the woods.

94
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:47,927
Rym: Gore and vore in this game.

95
00:07:51,420 --> 00:08:10,720
Emily: So, yeah, definitely don't necessarily take your first few minutes or the introductory title screen or anything like that as, oh, the game is just going to look like this and it has a humanoid princess and she's going to be like such and such.

96
00:08:11,100 --> 00:08:17,420
Emily: There's there's a lot of kind of spooky stuff, shall we say, in this game?

97
00:08:18,020 --> 00:08:19,325
Emily: And horror is definitely.

98
00:08:19,345 --> 00:08:23,199
Emily: I wouldn't say that horror is its primary thing.

99
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:24,827
Rym: Well, it can.

100
00:08:24,847 --> 00:08:26,679
Rym: that can be its primary thing.

101
00:08:27,343 --> 00:08:29,380
Rym: This game reacts to you as a player.

102
00:08:29,540 --> 00:08:47,160
Rym: And I guess where this succeeds, where a lot of visual novels and a lot of branching narrative games really fail for me is that it is meticulously designed so that every possible path is, I would say, equally satisfying, but more importantly, equally valid.

103
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:49,700
Rym: Like there's no correct path through this game.

104
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:54,179
Rym: A lot of visual novels will have the good path, the true path, the path you're expected to take.

105
00:08:55,101 --> 00:09:05,320
Rym: And the game even tells you in the very beginning, the game explicitly says there are no premature endings, meaning you can just play it through and you'll always get a full game no matter what decisions you make.

106
00:09:05,641 --> 00:09:09,260
Rym: You can't die in a way that prevents you from fully experiencing the game.

107
00:09:10,120 --> 00:09:15,800
Emily: I also think it's very interesting that it feels like it ends really soon.

108
00:09:16,740 --> 00:09:19,396
Emily: And then upside down, something happens again.

109
00:09:21,001 --> 00:09:26,952
Emily: So it's it's not necessarily a linear play experience.

110
00:09:27,072 --> 00:09:32,580
Emily: like your first quote, playthrough is not your first actual playthrough.

111
00:09:33,203 --> 00:09:47,400
Emily: So an interesting thing about this game, too, is that because there are chances to see some of the different branches, like you may have made one decision.

112
00:09:47,720 --> 00:09:48,918
Rym: You and I played the game.

113
00:09:49,120 --> 00:09:52,240
Emily: You will get a chance to make a different decision.

114
00:09:52,341 --> 00:09:55,500
Emily: In fact, the game forces you to make different decisions.

115
00:09:55,560 --> 00:09:58,700
Emily: You can't do the same thing multiple times.

116
00:09:59,040 --> 00:10:03,478
Rym: Well, that's part of the shtick that I think is worth calling out directly, because the game hints at it.

117
00:10:03,940 --> 00:10:07,140
Rym: But until you encounter it explicitly, it's not that obvious.

118
00:10:07,541 --> 00:10:14,660
Rym: The game, as soon as you get to a point to where the only decision you like, the decision you could make is one that you have already made exactly.

119
00:10:15,185 --> 00:10:16,200
Rym: And there would be no difference.

120
00:10:16,641 --> 00:10:27,999
Rym: It will prevent you from choosing something specifically forcing you to do literally anything differently from what you did the last time you played through the loop or the game, as it were.

121
00:10:28,542 --> 00:10:36,420
Emily: It's kind of interesting because we've been like, especially you have been watching a lot of quantum physics videos.

122
00:10:36,641 --> 00:10:41,720
Rym: that plays heavily into this, as does a lot of philosophy, philosophical schools.

123
00:10:42,140 --> 00:10:42,441
Emily: Yeah.

124
00:10:42,581 --> 00:10:48,160
Emily: So it's a deceptively simple premise at first.

125
00:10:48,501 --> 00:10:51,612
Emily: And like, you know, there's not a lot of it's.

126
00:10:52,234 --> 00:10:53,820
Emily: it's variations on a theme.

127
00:10:54,000 --> 00:11:02,940
Emily: The complexity comes not from like a bunch of different settings, like, you know, Baldur's Gate, you're down in the Underdark and you're over here and you're over there.

128
00:11:03,321 --> 00:11:05,860
Emily: No, there's like very few settings.

129
00:11:06,100 --> 00:11:10,740
Emily: There's, you know, the cabin in the woods, the woods, the basement of the cabin.

130
00:11:10,942 --> 00:11:13,340
Emily: And that's basically it for locations.

131
00:11:14,322 --> 00:11:17,960
Emily: And then there's you and the princess and the narrator.

132
00:11:18,602 --> 00:11:21,400
Emily: And those are a lot of characters.

133
00:11:21,500 --> 00:11:28,640
Emily: It might not seem like a lot of characters, but when I tell you like the princess is a lot of characters.

134
00:11:29,940 --> 00:11:40,340
Emily: This game is important to internalize that the variation comes from these simple elements being like all the different permutations.

135
00:11:40,620 --> 00:11:50,800
Rym: And I would say the game is playing with a lot of very basic and core themes very similarly to how things like Neon Genesis Evangelion are like.

136
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:55,778
Rym: They are talking about many of the same ideas just in a different way.

137
00:11:56,580 --> 00:12:12,959
Rym: And I guess what's really fascinating is you and I played very similarly, like we approached the situation largely similarly, but tiny, slight differences in decisions we made led to us seeing at some points radically different stories play out.

138
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:24,420
Emily: Well, it was very interesting because my literal first encounter was very different than your first encounter with the princess.

139
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:27,480
Emily: You know, she she spoke to me differently.

140
00:12:28,101 --> 00:12:30,620
Rym: Well, yeah, I was in and you did take the knife.

141
00:12:30,901 --> 00:12:32,800
Rym: I did take the knife and you went down to talk to her.

142
00:12:33,322 --> 00:12:35,700
Rym: I said basically said, fuck this.

143
00:12:35,841 --> 00:12:39,180
Rym: And I walked away from the cabin and found I found something.

144
00:12:40,120 --> 00:12:42,720
Emily: So that was that was an interesting thing.

145
00:12:43,040 --> 00:12:43,766
Rym: In fact, I got I got.

146
00:12:43,786 --> 00:12:44,008
Rym: I got.

147
00:12:44,028 --> 00:12:51,194
Rym: my first encounter with the princess was the princess saying something to the effect of I'm sorry, you encountered me first.

148
00:12:52,620 --> 00:13:00,160
Emily: Yeah, basically you you tried to break the the rules before I did.

149
00:13:00,380 --> 00:13:10,480
Emily: I was like, well, I'm going to follow and see what like the most standard play through happens.

150
00:13:11,100 --> 00:13:18,699
Emily: And so because we had the very different first approaches, we had very different experiences.

151
00:13:19,403 --> 00:13:22,580
Emily: But it was interesting because you said you wanted to see.

152
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:28,820
Emily: it was almost like a test to be like, let's see how Emily approaches this character and approaches this scenario.

153
00:13:29,422 --> 00:13:34,920
Emily: And actually, I think we ended up with a lot of the same because I didn't like super role play.

154
00:13:35,200 --> 00:13:37,780
Rym: I tried to put myself in the blank, kind of like with Disco Elysium.

155
00:13:37,860 --> 00:13:41,600
Rym: I put myself in the mind frame of, all right, this is all I know.

156
00:13:41,702 --> 00:13:42,397
Rym: What would I do?

157
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:50,520
Emily: See, the thing is, you in this game, in like Disco Elysium, you are playing a character and that character has a whole history.

158
00:13:50,880 --> 00:13:52,440
Rym: That character is very specific.

159
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:53,599
Rym: You just don't know it.

160
00:13:53,940 --> 00:13:56,020
Emily: So that's that's more role playing.

161
00:13:56,380 --> 00:14:05,137
Emily: I would say that Slay the Princess, it's it doesn't try to put much flavor on you as a player.

162
00:14:06,361 --> 00:14:12,020
Rym: The game even kind of says like diagetically, like in the context of the game.

163
00:14:12,682 --> 00:14:13,980
Rym: Look, buddy, no judgment.

164
00:14:14,343 --> 00:14:16,780
Rym: Well, you're not being judged by what you choose.

165
00:14:16,881 --> 00:14:22,539
Rym: But at the same time, I can imagine watching someone play through this game and judging them.

166
00:14:23,220 --> 00:14:24,719
Emily: So here's the thing.

167
00:14:25,140 --> 00:14:38,920
Emily: Like the player character is also referred to whenever you get to that specific scene where the princess tell you, you know, you're not supposed to see this yet, you know, but.

168
00:14:40,242 --> 00:14:43,760
Emily: That character is referred to as a vessel.

169
00:14:44,385 --> 00:14:45,899
Emily: You're just an empty vessel.

170
00:14:46,422 --> 00:14:54,317
Emily: And into that vessel goes not only the different narrative voices, but also you as the player.

171
00:14:55,080 --> 00:14:57,480
Emily: You have all the memories of playing this game.

172
00:14:57,660 --> 00:14:58,999
Emily: You know what's going to happen.

173
00:14:59,180 --> 00:15:00,260
Emily: You have all these.

174
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:04,198
Rym: And that's sort of the fun trick of the game, because you remember as a player.

175
00:15:05,503 --> 00:15:09,239
Rym: And it's clear that the voices in the character's head that you're playing remember.

176
00:15:09,922 --> 00:15:13,238
Rym: But the narrator does not remember it.

177
00:15:13,862 --> 00:15:27,600
Emily: It really kind of addresses directly the fact in gaming that in some ways, a character is an empty vessel into which the player pours their sense of agency.

178
00:15:27,900 --> 00:15:46,040
Rym: Coupled with this game is taking that and distilling it down to the simplest possible scenario of very direct binary choices and then really, really throwing the ramifications of those choices in your face and making you make increasingly difficult decisions.

179
00:15:46,541 --> 00:15:58,560
Emily: There are very few choices when you come right down to it, because if there were more choices, then it's exponential, because every choice matters and every choice leads to a radically different outcome.

180
00:15:59,163 --> 00:16:08,560
Emily: So I think it would be very difficult to make this game more complex in terms of your your decisions and having it be quite binary.

181
00:16:08,681 --> 00:16:14,620
Emily: Like, do you take the knife down to the basement where the princess is, or do you not take the knife down to the prince?

182
00:16:14,660 --> 00:16:24,959
Rym: And despite roleplaying myself and like kind of not trusting the narrator and trying to not be evil, I chose to bring the knife down there several times.

183
00:16:25,400 --> 00:16:28,559
Emily: Well, and sometimes, like I said, it forces you.

184
00:16:29,361 --> 00:16:31,738
Emily: You know, I'm just going to go right out and say it.

185
00:16:32,522 --> 00:16:42,998
Rym: There's a loop, you know, like there's a great moment you'll encounter early where if you try to not slay the princess, the game kind of tries to force you to.

186
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:43,721
Rym: Yes.

187
00:16:43,741 --> 00:16:48,000
Rym: And if you resist that happening, then you get a certain kind of scene.

188
00:16:48,482 --> 00:16:59,800
Rym: And if you go through the same scenario again and you still try to like not hurt the princess, you hit a point where the game says, all right, all your options, the option you chose last time is not available anymore.

189
00:17:00,540 --> 00:17:02,340
Rym: You have to do something bad.

190
00:17:03,080 --> 00:17:08,599
Emily: So there are two kinds, kind of like in Disco Elysium, there are two kinds of text, right?

191
00:17:08,761 --> 00:17:09,960
Emily: And two kinds of decisions.

192
00:17:10,482 --> 00:17:20,700
