Emily: Well, all the student council says it as they go up in the elevator.

592
00:46:52,640 --> 00:46:56,400
Rym: Oh, that's from Herman S. Demian.

593
00:46:56,930 --> 00:46:57,440
Emily: Yeah, Demian.

594
00:46:57,920 --> 00:47:03,140
Emily: And it's basically like the chick must smash itself to be born.

595
00:47:04,320 --> 00:47:11,180
Rym: From the story of a boyhood about a bird wrestling its way out of its egg.

596
00:47:11,360 --> 00:47:13,639
Rym: And really it's about boys coming of age.

597
00:47:15,103 --> 00:47:16,760
Emily: So I want to say we have that book.

598
00:47:16,882 --> 00:47:16,969
Emily: Yeah.

599
00:47:17,321 --> 00:47:22,200
Rym: I read it a long time ago when we were doing that show doing lit crit on Utena.

600
00:47:22,820 --> 00:47:22,913
Emily: Yeah.

601
00:47:23,321 --> 00:47:32,280
Emily: Anyway, there's another thing where it's like characters questioning their own reality and becoming more because they're kind of trapped within a cycle.

602
00:47:32,700 --> 00:47:48,039
Rym: But ultimately in the end, I like that we both, when you're confronted with the full reality of the constructs and you have to decide what to do, we both basically decided to break out into absolute reality with the princess knowing everything she could be.

603
00:47:48,942 --> 00:47:52,660
Rym: I think I'd rather... Go out alone.

604
00:47:52,800 --> 00:47:54,280
Rym: And I wasn't going to stay in my egg forever.

605
00:47:55,266 --> 00:47:56,440
Emily: But that's the question.

606
00:47:56,660 --> 00:47:58,430
Emily: You know, it's like, would you rather live?

607
00:48:01,144 --> 00:48:02,600
Emily: Would you rather live, period?

608
00:48:03,200 --> 00:48:07,679
Emily: Like living is a certain amount of suffering, a certain amount of imperfection.

609
00:48:08,141 --> 00:48:15,240
Emily: Would you rather like static, unchanging, angelic, like nothingness?

610
00:48:15,762 --> 00:48:18,580
Emily: Or would you rather the messy chaos of existence?

611
00:48:18,721 --> 00:48:26,780
Rym: Well, it goes further, too, because that is the same dichotomy that Neon Genesis Evangelion delves in in the original show.

612
00:48:27,461 --> 00:48:29,894
Rym: And in the remake, it triples down on that.

613
00:48:29,954 --> 00:48:33,280
Rym: when you get to see minor spoilers for Evangelion.

614
00:48:33,781 --> 00:48:39,480
Rym: You basically see the universe as Gendo wants it and what it would be if Gendo achieved everything he wanted.

615
00:48:39,941 --> 00:48:44,600
Rym: And it's just him alone in a giant, cool ship being a sad sack for all of eternity.

616
00:48:45,034 --> 00:48:45,240
Emily: Yeah.

617
00:48:45,540 --> 00:48:53,620
Emily: So it's basically the whole point of Neon Genesis Evangelion was basically, you know, the hedgehog's dilemma.

618
00:48:54,180 --> 00:48:58,100
Emily: Vulnerability and letting people in often hurts you.

619
00:48:58,302 --> 00:48:59,480
Emily: You know, it's difficult.

620
00:48:59,560 --> 00:49:02,960
Emily: You can never truly connect because we're all individuals.

621
00:49:03,582 --> 00:49:07,360
Emily: But you have to try to let people in.

622
00:49:07,440 --> 00:49:14,760
Emily: You have to try to connect and be a part of the world instead of walling yourself off in your own little cell.

623
00:49:15,000 --> 00:49:37,759
Rym: But the other thing it evokes very strongly is the the singularity versus duality concept that appears in a lot of philosophy and a lot of literature in the sense that some of what the construct says, depending on what you ask, basically implies that there was one god and the person who made the construct basically clove that god in two and wanted to kill half of it.

624
00:49:38,281 --> 00:49:40,449
Rym: But that implies, too, that your choice is.

625
00:49:40,509 --> 00:49:50,519
Rym: that is is also is there just one thing or are there two things that might not agree or possibly more things, which is very close to, again, what Evangelion was all out about?

626
00:49:51,380 --> 00:49:52,398
Emily: Wouldn't you say that?

627
00:49:52,980 --> 00:50:00,340
Emily: So if you consider that the player character is a god, what do you think they are god of?

628
00:50:00,942 --> 00:50:02,440
Rym: That is complicated.

629
00:50:02,942 --> 00:50:12,600
Emily: Yeah, like you you talked a lot about the mound, about being a singularity, about being natural selection, although what do you think you the player represents?

630
00:50:12,700 --> 00:50:15,760
Rym: I mean, the game seems to imply that you are effectively death.

631
00:50:16,420 --> 00:50:17,949
Rym: You are described as the long quiet.

632
00:50:21,280 --> 00:50:25,300
Emily: Oh, you know, in some ways that really makes sense, because if the princess is...

633
00:50:25,360 --> 00:50:27,860
Rym: Between every act, you go back to the long quiet.

634
00:50:27,980 --> 00:50:30,740
Rym: At the end, the narrator calls you the long quiet.

635
00:50:30,841 --> 00:50:32,239
Rym: It basically says that's your name.

636
00:50:32,880 --> 00:50:39,580
Emily: In some ways, you could kind of think about it like, if there's no life, there's also no death.

637
00:50:39,902 --> 00:50:41,472
Emily: You know, how do we end death?

638
00:50:41,653 --> 00:50:42,600
Emily: by ending life?

639
00:50:43,240 --> 00:50:44,676
Rym: So you can be immortal and unchanging.

640
00:50:45,880 --> 00:50:51,820
Emily: Yeah, by getting rid of change, by getting rid of the living things, then there is no more death.

641
00:50:51,840 --> 00:50:54,660
Rym: But also the mound can't evolve without death.

642
00:50:54,700 --> 00:51:02,479
Rym: And that's why I come back to that natural selection idea, because it reminded me of that story that had a recent fairly well animated YouTube video, The Goddess of Everything Else.

643
00:51:03,180 --> 00:51:16,160
Rym: And the idea that if the construct only works because you don't understand what's happening, once you understand it and you emerge into the world as a god, it's you and the mound are the two things in the universe effectively.

644
00:51:16,620 --> 00:51:21,119
Rym: And at that point, you can interact with each other, but the mound is as immortal as you are.

645
00:51:22,060 --> 00:51:27,120
Rym: So there can still be change without necessarily death, but that's a different mode of being.

646
00:51:27,221 --> 00:51:29,820
Rym: It's almost like transcending the previous order.

647
00:51:30,880 --> 00:51:32,940
Emily: Well, death changes life.

648
00:51:33,320 --> 00:51:39,400
Rym: I mean, imagine a distant future where conscious sentience evolves without ever having to die, but changes radically.

649
00:51:39,961 --> 00:51:49,840
Rym: And, you know, the classic story of the thousand year old elf, who is a fundamentally different person now, but is one contiguous chain of experience and memories, but it still changes.

650
00:51:50,240 --> 00:51:53,760
Rym: But it's a different kind of death from, well, you're just dead.

651
00:51:54,520 --> 00:51:55,187
Emily: Gone forever.

652
00:51:55,207 --> 00:51:56,520
Emily: Think no thoughts anymore.

653
00:51:56,600 --> 00:51:58,271
Rym: What child that you had.

654
00:51:58,412 --> 00:52:03,658
Rym: or your memes, maybe infect other people down the road, but you don't get to experience any of that.

655
00:52:04,602 --> 00:52:05,539
Emily: Interesting stuff.

656
00:52:06,549 --> 00:52:15,740
Rym: And on that note, sketchily drawn visual novel, body horror, eldritch story game.

657
00:52:16,601 --> 00:52:18,898
Emily: I think it was well worth under 20 bucks.

658
00:52:19,640 --> 00:52:24,500
Emily: You know, I would definitely recommend this game if any of this stuff sounded interesting.

659
00:52:24,783 --> 00:52:33,180
Emily: And I'm assuming if you've listened this far, you're either never going to play the game and you just want to hear a CMR about it, or you have played the game and wanted to hear other people.

660
00:52:33,320 --> 00:52:35,980
Rym: I hope if you haven't played the game that you're at least intrigued.

661
00:52:37,084 --> 00:52:44,700
Emily: Yeah, you should still play it, even though we've spoiled some stuff and told you about the stuff behind the curtain.

662
00:52:45,000 --> 00:52:46,740
Rym: Yeah, because mechanically, one thing the game does well.

663
00:52:47,000 --> 00:52:49,140
Emily: Break down the set and go behind it.

664
00:52:49,280 --> 00:52:53,720
Rym: Every choice you can click on matters and does something interesting.

665
00:52:53,924 --> 00:52:54,740
Rym: Nothing is boring.

666
00:52:54,860 --> 00:52:58,940
Rym: There is literally no direction you can go where nothing uninteresting happens.

667
00:52:59,160 --> 00:53:00,038
Rym: There's always something.

668
00:53:00,820 --> 00:53:01,800
Rym: Yeah, highly recommended.

669
00:53:02,240 --> 00:53:04,380
Rym: A surprising number of options in some of these stories.

670
